"""Hand-written English method descriptions, part 6: equipment Barbell.

Keyed by the v5 catalog slug (slugify of the canonical exercise name).
Merged by build_progression.py as the last description tier; a key that
matches no catalog row fails the build, so typos surface immediately.
"""

DESCRIPTIONS = {
    "barbell_calf_raise": "The barbell calf raise stands with a bar on the back and drives the heels up through a full ankle extension. Keep the knees straight to bias the gastrocnemius, pause at the top and lower slowly through the full stretch; balance near a rack for safety.",
    "barbell_overhead_extension": "The barbell overhead extension lowers a bar behind the head from a locked-out overhead grip and extends it back up, working the long head of the triceps through a deep stretch. Keep the elbows pointing forward and narrow, and the ribs down.",
    "barbell_overhead_press": "The barbell overhead press presses a barbell from the shoulders to lockout with the legs quiet, the purest measure of upper-body pressing strength. Squeeze the glutes to lock the torso, move the head back out of the bar's path and finish with the bar over the mid-foot.",
    "barbell_pullover": "The barbell pullover arcs a bar from over the chest to behind the head and back while lying on a bench, stretching the lats and chest under load. Keep the elbows slightly bent and the hips steady; the movement happens at the shoulders, not the spine.",
    "barbell_rear_delt_row": "The barbell rear delt row rows a barbell high toward the chest with the elbows flared wide, biasing the rear delts and upper back over the lats. Hinge to near-parallel and keep the neck long; think of pulling with the elbows, not the hands.",
    "barbell_wrist_curl": "The barbell wrist curl rests the forearms on a bench or the thighs and hinges the wrists to curl the bar up, training the forearm flexors. Use a modest weight and a full range, from fully extended fingers to full flexion; slow negatives build the grip fastest.",
    "behind_the_neck_press": "The behind-the-neck press presses a barbell from behind the head to lockout, demanding exceptional shoulder mobility. It builds the overhead position powerfully but is unforgiving of tight shoulders; only press from behind the neck if the bar can travel there without the head jutting forward.",
    "bench_pull": "The bench pull lies chest-down on a high bench and rows a barbell from beneath it, removing all body English from the pull. The chest stays planted so the lats and mid-back do the work; pull the bar to the underside of the bench and lower with control.",
    "bent_arm_ez_bar_pullover": "The bent-arm EZ-bar pullover lies on a bench and arcs the bar from over the chest to behind the head with the elbows bent, easing the demand on the shoulders while still stretching the lats and chest. Keep the elbows pointing up and the ribs down throughout.",
    "bent_over_ez_bar_row": "The bent-over EZ-bar row hinges forward and rows the angled bar to the lower ribs, the EZ grip turning the wrists into a more natural position for many lifters. Keep the torso close to parallel and drive the elbows past the ribs.",
    "cheat_curl": "The cheat curl uses a controlled body swing to start a heavier-than-strict barbell curl, then fights the lowering with pure biceps. The cheat is the setup, not the rep, and the eccentric must stay strict; it is an advanced overload tool, not a permission slip to swing every curl.",
    "clean": "The clean pulls a barbell from the floor to the shoulders in one explosive motion, catching it on the front delts in a quarter squat. It develops triple-extension power through the hips, knees and ankles unlike any slow lift; the pull is vertical and the elbows whip under fast.",
    "close_grip_incline_bench_press": "The close-grip incline bench press presses from an incline bench with the hands inside shoulder width, stacking the triceps and upper chest together. Keep the elbows tucked close to the torso and touch the bar just below the collar line.",
    "decline_ez_bar_bench_press": "The decline EZ-bar bench press presses from a decline bench with the angled bar, biasing the lower chest while easing shoulder strain. Keep the feet hooked, the bar path over the lower chest and the elbows about 45 degrees from the torso.",
    "ez_bar_bench_press": "The EZ-bar bench press benches with the angled bar, trading a little pec load for a far friendlier wrist and elbow position. Grip the inner bends, keep the forearms vertical at the bottom and drive the bar up in a slight arc over the chest.",
    "ez_bar_front_raise": "The EZ-bar front raise lifts the angled bar from the thighs to shoulder height, training the front delts with a wrist-friendly grip. Keep the elbows nearly straight and the torso still; raise only to shoulder height, since momentum beyond that is wasted.",
    "ez_bar_lying_triceps_extension": "The EZ-bar lying triceps extension lowers the angled bar to the forehead or slightly behind from a locked-out press, isolating the triceps under a deep stretch. Keep the elbows fixed in space pointing at the ceiling; only the forearms travel.",
    "ez_bar_overhead_tricep_extension": "The EZ-bar overhead tricep extension lowers the angled bar behind the head from an overhead lockout, stretching the long head of the triceps fully. Keep the elbows narrow and pointing forward, and the ribcage down; the stretch is the working part of the rep.",
    "ez_bar_pullover": "The EZ-bar pullover arcs the angled bar from the chest to beyond the head while lying down, stretching the lats and ribcage under load. The angled grip spares the shoulders that a straight bar punishes; keep the hips planted and the arms softly bent.",
    "ez_bar_reverse_grip_row": "The EZ-bar reverse-grip row rows the bar to the lower ribs with palms up, shifting the pull toward the lats and biceps. Keep the torso hinged close to parallel and the elbows tracking close to the body; squeeze the shoulder blades at the top.",
    "ez_bar_romanian_deadlift": "The EZ-bar Romanian deadlift hinges the hips back under the angled bar, stretching the hamstrings to their working end and driving back up. The angled grip suits stiff wrists and lets the load sit closer; keep the bar grazing the legs and the spine long.",
    "ez_bar_upright_row": "The EZ-bar upright row pulls the angled bar up the front of the body to chest height, working the side delts and traps through a wrist-neutral grip. Keep the elbows above the hands and stop before the pull pinches the shoulder; moderate weight, strict path.",
    "ez_bar_wrist_curl": "The EZ-bar wrist curl flexes the wrists under the angled bar with the forearms braced, training the flexors through the most wrist-comfortable angle available. Full range beats heavy weight here: let the bar roll to the fingertips and curl it back up.",
    "floor_ez_bar_press": "The floor EZ-bar press benches from the floor, which stops the elbows at a safe depth while the angled bar spares the wrists. It hammers the lockout half of the press and the triceps; pause when the upper arms settle before driving up.",
    "floor_press": "The floor press presses a barbell from the floor with the shoulder blades pinned, cutting the range at the point the shoulders would take over. It builds raw triceps and lockout strength and spares cranky shoulders; let the triceps rest briefly on the floor between reps.",
    "front_squat": "The front squat squats with the bar racked on the front delts, keeping the torso upright and shifting load toward the quads and upper back. The elbows stay high and the wrists extended; it is the most technically honest of the heavy squats, and a weak position shows immediately.",
    "hang_clean": "The hang clean pulls the bar from above the knee to the shoulders in one explosive extension, trading the floor pull for pure power from the hang. Keep the bar close as the hips extend, then whip the elbows under into the front rack.",
    "hang_power_clean": "The hang power clean catches the bar above parallel in a shallow dip, pulling from the hang and landing tall. It is the most repeatable clean variation for power work; the same crisp extension with less demand on the front-rack mobility.",
    "heel_elevated_squat": "The heel-elevated squat raises the heels on a plate or wedge and squats, letting the knees travel further forward to load the quads through a longer range. Keep the torso upright and sink between the feet; the depth the wedge buys is the point.",
    "incline_ez_bar_bench_press": "The incline EZ-bar bench press presses from an incline bench with the angled bar, biasing the upper chest while easing wrist and elbow strain. Keep the bar path over the collar line, the elbows about 45 degrees from the torso and the shoulder blades pinched.",
    "landmine_press": "The landmine press presses the end of a barbell set in a landmine attachment or corner up and forward from the shoulder. The angled path spares the shoulder joint while still loading the delts and chest; step into it and drive through the front foot.",
    "muscle_snatch": "The muscle snatch pulls the bar overhead without any rebend of the knees or elbows, keeping the arms straight through the finish. It is a strength and position drill for the snatch; go lighter than a power snatch and punch the bar up, then lock it hard.",
    "one_arm_landmine_press": "The one-arm landmine press presses the angled bar with a single arm from the shoulder, training each side while the core resists the twist. The landmine's path lets the shoulder blade move freely; stand staggered and press up and forward along the bar's line.",
    "overhead_squat": "The overhead squat squats with a bar locked out overhead, demanding mobility, stability and strength from ankles to shoulders at once. It is the deepest test of squat mechanics in the gym; keep the bar over the mid-foot throughout and treat light weights as technique work.",
    "pause_deadlift": "The pause deadlift stops the pull below the knee for a full breath before finishing, killing momentum and exposing weak positions. Keep the lats engaged and the hips high through the pause; the restart must come from the legs, not a yank.",
    "pause_squat": "The pause squat sits in the hole for two to three seconds before driving up, removing the stretch reflex and forcing pure strength out of the bottom. Stay tight and keep the brace during the pause; the first inch of the ascent is where the rep is won.",
    "paused_bench_press": "The paused bench press holds the bar dead on the chest for a beat before pressing, killing the bounce and building strength from a dead stop. Keep the shoulder blades pinched and the torso tight through the pause; touch, hold, then drive.",
    "paused_incline_bench_press": "The paused incline bench press holds the bar dead on the upper chest from an incline bench before pressing, removing momentum from the hardest pressing angle. Keep the wrists stacked and the pause quiet, with no sinking into the rep.",
    "paused_overhead_press": "The paused overhead press holds the bar at chin or forehead height before pressing to lockout, eliminating the leg-drive bounce and building strength through the sticking point. Keep the torso braced and the bar path straight through the pause.",
    "reverse_curl": "The reverse curl curls a barbell with the palms facing down, shifting the work from the biceps to the brachialis and forearm extensors. Keep the elbows pinned and the wrists straight, since curling with bent wrists cheats the forearms out of the rep.",
    "seated_barbell_overhead_press": "The seated barbell overhead press presses from an upright bench, removing leg drive so the shoulders do every bit of the work. Keep the lower back against the pad and the bar path just in front of the face; expect the strictest overhead strength test in the rack.",
    "snatch": "The snatch takes the barbell from the floor to overhead in one continuous motion, catching it in a deep overhead squat. It is the most athletic lift in the barbell catalog, demanding speed, mobility and precision in one; the bar must stay close and the punch overhead violent.",
    "split_jerk": "The split jerk drives the bar overhead with a dip and a split of the legs, dropping under the bar to catch it with straight arms. It moves more overhead weight than any press; recover the feet front, back, together, and keep the front shin vertical.",
    "stiff_leg_deadlift": "The stiff-leg deadlift hinges at the hips with only a soft knee bend, loading the hamstrings through a longer stretch than the conventional pull. Keep the bar close to the legs and the spine long; the range ends where the hamstrings stop, not where the plates touch.",
    "sumo_squat": "The sumo squat squats with a very wide stance and the toes turned out, shifting load toward the adductors, glutes and inner quads. Keep the knees tracking over the toes and the torso tall; sink between the hips rather than folding forward.",
    "t_bar_row": "The T-bar row rows a landmine-loaded bar from a hinged stance, pulling the weighted end to the chest with both hands. The anchored end removes balance demands so the lats and mid-back can be loaded hard; keep the hinge still and the chest proud.",
    "thruster": "The thruster chains a front squat into an overhead press in one continuous movement, using the squat's drive to punch the bar up. It is the ultimate full-body conditioning lift; ride the bar down into the next squat smoothly and breathe at the top.",
}
